{
  "image_name": "35235aab16e9db1d3af89fc9c7cc1888.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several strategies for controlling biofilms and corrosion on metal surfaces, especially in pipelines and related systems. Across the panels, the common theme is either removing attached microbes, killing them at the surface, or creating a barrier that prevents both microbial growth and corrosion.\n\nSome approaches are mechanical or chemical, such as pipeline pigging combined with a biocide and agents that disperse established biofilms. Others rely on antimicrobial materials: a metal alloy appears to release ions such as copper, silver, and cerium that distinguish live from dead cells, while coatings either passively cover the metal or act as “smart” layers that release healing or antimicrobial agents when damaged. Another set of methods uses antibiofilm chemicals, including biologically produced compounds, chelators, and surfactants.\n\nBiological control is also shown through phage therapy, where bacteriophages infect bacteria, replicate, and cause cell lysis. A bifunctional corrosion inhibitor seems to combine a corrosion barrier with antimicrobial activity at the metal interface. Finally, a protective biofilm is depicted as beneficial, forming an EPS and biomineralization layer that blocks oxygen and excludes corrosive microbes, thereby reducing oxygen-driven corrosion.",
